==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtɪlə/}}
* {{rhymes|ɪlə(r)}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{suffix|till|er}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A person who [[till]]s; a [[farmer]].
#* '''2000''', {{w|Alasdair Gray}}, ''The Book of Prefaces'', Bloomsbury 2002, p. 63:
#*: In France, Europe's most fertile and cultivated land, the '''tillers''' of it suffered more and more hunger.
# A machine that mechanically tills the soil.

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm}} *{{term||tilȝer, *telȝer|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|telgor|lang=ang}}, {{term|telgra|lang=ang}}, {{term|telgre|lang=ang}} {{gloss|"twig, branch, shoot"; also {{term|telga|lang=ang}}, {{term|telge|lang=ang}} > {{l|en|tillow}}}}, from {{proto|Germanic|telgô||telgōn|twig, branch|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|delgʰ-|to split, divide, cut, carve|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|telg||descendant, scion, offshoot, shoot|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|nds|-}} {{term|telge||twig, branch|lang=nds}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Zelge||twig, branch, bough|lang=de}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|telning||branch, scion, sapling|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|tág||willow-twig|lang=is}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|tillow}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{obsolete}} A young [[tree]].
# A [[shoot]] of a [[plant]] which springs from the root or bottom of the original [[stalk]]; a [[sapling]]; a [[sucker]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{intransitive}} To put forth new [[shoot]]s from the root or from around the bottom of the original stalk; stool.

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|put forth new shoots}}
* Italian: {{t-|it|accestire}}
{{trans-mid}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 3===
{{etyl|xno}} {{term|telier||beam used in weaving|lang=xno}}, from {{etyl|ML.}} {{term|telarium|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|tela||web|lang=la}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{archery}} The [[stock]]; a beam on a crossbow carved to fit the [[arrow]], or the point of balance in a longbow.
# {{nautical}} A [[bar]] of iron or wood connected with the [[rudderhead]] and [[leadline]], usually [[forward]], in which the [[rudder]] is moved as desired by the tiller (FM 55-501).
# {{nautical}} The handle of the [[rudder]] which the [[helmsman]] holds to [[steer]] the boat, a piece of wood or metal extending forward from the rudder over or through the [[transom]]. Generally attached at the top of the rudder.
# A [[handle]]; a [[stalk]].

